God.For {elohim,} God, Houbigant reads {aleyhem,} unto them;
which is countenanced by the LXX., Vulgate, Chaldee, Coptic,
and Arabic, and appears to have been the original reading.

brass.{Nechosheth,} rather, copper; as brass is a factitious metal,
composed of copper, and the oxide or ore of zinc, called
{lapis calaminaris.}

blue.{Techaileth,} generally supposed to mean an azure or
sky-colour; rendered by the LXX, [uakinthon,] {uakinthon},
and Vulgate, {hyacinthum.}

emerald.{Nophech,} an emerald, the same with the ancient {smaragdus;}
one of the most beautiful of all the gems, and of a bright
green colour, without any mixture.

bracelets.{Chach,} either a hook or clasp to join garments together;
{fibula,} as Montanus renders; or bracelets, which are hooked
or clasped together; so Vulgate, {armillas.}

fine.The word {Shesh} seems rather to mean cotton, than either
fine linen or silk; called also {butz,} and by the Greeks,
[byssos,] and Romans, {byssus.}
